---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Publishing Services — Silver Sage Media"
        description="Silver Sage Media offers publishing infrastructure, distribution stack setup, affiliate operations, and launch corridor management for independent authors and small publishers. Three engagement models — Full-Service, Infrastructure Only, and Consultative.">

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section style="max-width:780px;">
    <p class="section-label">Publishing Services</p>
    <h2>Silver Sage Publishing Services</h2>
    <p class="section-intro" style="font-style:italic;">One person built five publishing properties. We'll show you how the infrastructure works — and help you build yours.</p>

    <div class="about-text" style="margin-top:2rem;">
      <p>Silver Sage Media is a publishing and distribution operation running on a stack of AI-native tools, automated workflows, and hard-won process. We've launched books through KDP and IngramSpark, built affiliate programs with FTC-compliant disclosure architecture, managed ARC campaigns and pre-launch buzz from a single operator's desk, and run Substack newsletters across multiple brands simultaneously — all without an agency, a marketing team, or a budget that would make a traditional publisher laugh.</p>

      <p>That infrastructure is now available to independent authors and small publishers who want to do the same.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- What We Do -->
  <section>
    <h2>What We Do</h2>
    <div class="grid-3">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Publishing Infrastructure</h3>
        <p class="sans">Setting up a book for success before it launches involves more paperwork than writing it. We handle KDP and IngramSpark setup, ISBN assignment and management, pricing configuration across markets, and the technical backend that keeps your book available and properly formatted wherever readers buy.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Distribution Stack</h3>
        <p class="sans">Getting review copies to the right people, listed on the right platforms, at the right time is its own project. We manage BookFunnel and BookSirens setups, NetGalley listings, and advance copy coordination — the unglamorous logistics that determine whether your launch has any reviews when it goes live.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Affiliate &amp; Revenue Operations</h3>
        <p class="sans">The difference between a link in a book recommendation and a compliant, traceable affiliate program is one that most independent authors never close. We build affiliate architectures with proper FTC disclosure language, working telemetry, and partner management — so you know what's converting and can defend your disclosure posture if anyone asks.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Launch Corridor Management</h3>
        <p class="sans">Launch day is the end of a multi-week operation, not the beginning of one. We run pre-launch buzz campaigns, ARC distribution, day-of publication orchestration, and the first week of post-launch monitoring — the window where the algorithm decisions that follow your book for the next year are being made.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- How We Work -->
  <section>
    <h2>How We Work</h2>
    <p class="section-intro" style="max-width:750px;">We offer three engagement models. What's right for you depends on how much of the stack you want built versus run.</p>

    <div class="grid-3" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Full-Service</h3>
        <p class="sans">We take on the whole publishing infrastructure — setup, operations, and launch corridor — so you can focus on the writing.</p>
        <p class="sans" style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Best fit:</strong> debut authors with no existing distribution stack who want to start right.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Infrastructure Only</h3>
        <p class="sans">We build the stack and hand it to you. KDP/IngramSpark accounts configured, BookFunnel and affiliate programs set up, process documented. You run it; we did the setup.</p>
        <p class="sans" style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Best fit:</strong> authors who want control but don't want to reverse-engineer the architecture.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Consultative</h3>
        <p class="sans">You have an existing operation. Something isn't working, or you're about to do something new. We review your setup, identify the gaps, and give you a specific recommendation — not a generic checklist.</p>
        <p class="sans" style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Best fit:</strong> publishers who've launched before and want a second set of eyes.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="about-text" style="max-width:750px;margin-top:2rem;">
      <p>Pricing is set project-by-project based on what you're actually building. We're currently accepting a small number of new engagements. If you have a launch window in 2026, start the conversation now.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Why Silver Sage -->
  <section>
    <h2>Why Silver Sage</h2>
    <div class="about-text" style="max-width:750px;">
      <p>We don't consult from theory. Every service we offer is something we run in production for our own properties. OGF, GMA, EEC, and TUP are live publishing operations — Substack newsletters, print books, affiliate programs, launch campaigns — and our debut novel, <em>The Lund Covenant</em>, is <a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0H1XQCBPH">available for pre-order now</a>, launching June 30, 2026. All of it maintained by one person (Greg Maxfield) using the same infrastructure we'd build for you.</p>

      <p>That's a different claim than "we've helped 200 authors." It's: <em>we run this, every week, for real, and we're willing to show you exactly how.</em></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <section id="contact">
    <h2>Tell Us What You're Working On</h2>
    <div class="about-text" style="max-width:750px;">
      <p>If you're planning a launch in the next six months, or you're running a publishing operation that's outgrown the approach you started with, we'd like to hear about it.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="contact-grid" style="margin-top:2rem;">
      <div class="contact-form">
        <label>Name</label>
        <input type="text" placeholder="Your name" />
        <label>Email</label>
        <input type="email" placeholder="your@email.com" />
        <label>What are you working on?</label>
        <input type="text" placeholder="Book launch, infrastructure setup, second-opinion review, etc." />
        <label>Tell us more</label>
        <textarea placeholder="Launch window, current setup, what's not working, what you want built..."></textarea>
        <button class="btn btn-primary" style="margin-top:1.5rem;" onclick="window.location.href='mailto:greg@silversagemedia.com?subject=Publishing%20Services%20Inquiry'">Send Inquiry</button>
        <p class="sans" style="font-size:0.8rem;margin-top:1rem;opacity:0.7;">Or email <a href="mailto:greg@silversagemedia.com">greg@silversagemedia.com</a> directly.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="contact-info">
        <h3>What to Expect</h3>
        <p class="sans">We'll respond within 3 business days. No pitch decks. No discovery calls that turn into discovery calls.</p>
        <p class="sans" style="margin-top:1rem;"><strong>Direct email:</strong> <a href="mailto:greg@silversagemedia.com">greg@silversagemedia.com</a></p>
        <p class="sans"><strong>Location:</strong> Camas, Washington</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Layout>
